  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nWhen you look at an upper level chart you can see about a 600dm\nridge at 500mb centered just to the southwest of the area. The\nthermometer at the surface apparently sees the same thing as our\ntemperatures are approaching the mid 90s across the southern\nhalf of the area and upper 80s in the northeast. Dewpoints are\nmixing down, mercifully, just a tiny bit into the mid 70s. Heat\nindex values are still over 105 or headed that way amid clear\nskies. With the deep mixing LCL heights are up over 8000 ft\nwhich will significantly limit any prospects of showers or\nstorms this afternoon.\n\nTomorrow looks just as hot with max apparent Ts in the 105-110\nrange. The cumulative heat of this extended streak will continue\nto make for a dangerous situation. Our newer \"Heat Risk\" tool \nstill shows level 3 and 4 (out of 4) categorization across the \nentire CWA.\n\nA zonal jet max to the north finally builds far enough south\nSunday to nudge a cold front through Sunday night into Monday.\nDeep mixing produces very high LCL heights again tomorrow and\nthat hot (relatively drier) air near the surface may limit\nparcel buoyancy somewhat, but 1500-2000 J/kg of MLCAPE looks to\nstill be available when the front pushes through giving us\ndecent shower and thunderstorm chances if the front can lift the\nparcels high enough to get going. If they do the potential for\nlocalized downbursts/winds will be there given the high 0-3km\ntheta-e difference and low level inverted V/DCAPE.\n\nWednesday and Thursday a shortwave trough works off the Rockies\nand into the central plains and upper midwest. This kicks up\nflow out of the southwest and the lift and moisture look likely\nto combine to produce shower and thunderstorm chances.\nDeterministic GFS is a little sharper with this wave but the\nECMWF also has it as do most ensemble members. Will need to\nwatch for some severe weather potential if that signal remains\ngiven the lift/deep shear available with a warm and humid\nairmass. Low level shear actually creeps up just a bit in the\nGFS deterministic run. CSU-ML and CIPS analogs are also\nflickering just a bit over that period as well. Once the\nshortwave passes a cold front seems to have a respectable chance\nto make it into the area by the weekend. The current\ndeterministic forecast has highs in the mid 80s with dewpoints\nin the mid 60s, which would feel positively Baltic compared to \nwhat we have seen for the last week!",
